<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euthymius II of Novgorod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1458">1458</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Evfimy II, Saint Euthymius of Novgorod</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euthymius II was Archbishop of Novgorod from 1429 to 1458 and one of the most powerful figures of the late Novgorod Republic. He sponsored extensive building and restoration in the city, including the Palace of Facets in the archbishop's court, built in 1433, and he promoted the copying of chronicles and the cult of local saints. He was later canonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euthymius-ii-of-novgorod.jpg" alt="Euthymius II of Novgorod" width="318" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evfimiy_of_Novgorod.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his saints' lives, Evfimy was baptised Ioann or Ivan, the son of a priest, Fedor, and his wife Anna; some lives name his father Mikheia and make him priest of the Church of St. Fedor the Great Martyr on the Market side of Novgorod. He became a monk at the Listitsa Hill Monastery and later moved to the Vyazhishchsky Monastery north-west of the city, from which he is also known as Evfimy Viazhishchskii.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The veche elected him archbishop after the death of Evfimy I in 1429, but he was not consecrated until 1434, and then not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> but in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> by Geronty, the Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, which angered the Muscovite authorities. Much of his patronage looked back to Novgorod's past: he built or rebuilt more than a dozen churches on their old foundations and in the old styles, among them the Church of John the Forerunner at Opoky, first built by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-mstislavich">Vsevolod Mstislavich</a> in 1127. He also built a belfry east of the Cathedral of Holy Wisdom and a clocktower west of it.</p><p>In 1439 he uncovered the relics of Archbishop Ilya and commemorated him with eight other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and archbishops seen in a vision that year. He brought Pachomius the Serb to Novgorod, who wrote the Life of Varlaam of Khutyn and the Tale of Ilya's journey on a devil to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1456 Grand Prince Vasily II defeated Novgorod and imposed the peace of Iazhelbitsy, which limited its foreign policy. Evfimy is not named in the treaty and built no churches after that year; he may have withdrawn. He died on 11 March 1458 and was buried at the Viazhishchskii Monastery. Pachomius later wrote his Life, and the Moscow Council of 1549 canonised him. His remains lie in the monastery's Church of St. Evfimy, dedicated to him in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Euthymius II of Novgorod” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1448" aria-label="Veliky Novgorod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5214 190 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Euthymius II of Novgorod"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5664 460h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5664 460h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1448">What was here in 1448? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
